LAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n750,HISTOGRAM,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Histogram\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nA histogram is a graphical representation that displays the distribution of numerical data by dividing the data into intervals, known as bins, and counting the number of observations that fall within each bin. Its primary purpose is to provide a visual summary of the data distribution, allowing researchers to assess the shape, central tendency, and variability of the data. Histograms are particularly useful for checking the normality of residuals in statistical analyses, such as regression and ANOVA, and for summarizing experimental data, such as tension bond strength.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nWhile a histogram does not have a formal mathematical model like some statistical tests, it can be described in terms of its construction:  \n- Let \\( x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\) be a set of numerical observations.  \n- The range of the data is divided into \\( k \\) intervals (bins), where each bin \\( [a_i, b_i) \\) contains a count \\( f_i \\) of observations.  \n- The height of each bar in the histogram represents the frequency \\( f_i \\) or relative frequency \\( \\frac{f_i}{n} \\) of observations in that bin.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nHistograms are typically structured with:\n- The x-axis representing the bins (intervals of data).\n- The y-axis representing the frequency or count of observations within each bin.\n- Bars that are adjacent to each other, indicating the continuous nature of the data.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nHistograms do not have strict assumptions like parametric tests; however, they are most informative when:\n- The data is continuous and numerical.\n- The sample size is sufficiently large to provide a meaningful representation of the distribution.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nHistograms are applicable in various scenarios, including:\n- Visualizing the distribution of a dataset to identify patterns, outliers, or skewness.\n- Checking the normality of residuals in regression analysis or ANOVA.\n- Summarizing experimental data, such as tension bond strength, to facilitate interpretation.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis procedure for creating a histogram involves:\n1. Collecting the numerical data.\n2. Determining the number of bins and their width.\n3. Counting the frequency of observations in each bin.\n4. Plotting the frequencies against the bins to create the histogram.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Provides a clear visual representation of data distribution.\n- Facilitates the identification of patterns, trends, and outliers.\n- Simple to construct and interpret, making it accessible for various audiences.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- The choice of bin width and number of bins can significantly affect the appearance and interpretation of the histogram.\n- Histograms may obscure details in the data if the binning is not appropriately chosen.\n- They do not provide specific statistical information, such as mean or variance, without further analysis.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nHistograms are related to other graphical representations such as:\n- Box plots, which summarize data through quartiles and highlight outliers.\n- Density plots, which provide a smoothed estimate of the data distribution.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing histograms, researchers should consider:\n- The appropriate selection of bin width and number of bins to accurately represent the data.\n- The potential need for data transformation if the data is heavily skewed.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nHistograms can be easily created using statistical software packages such as R, Python (with libraries like Matplotlib and Seaborn), SPSS, and Excel, which provide built-in functions for generating histograms.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nHistograms are commonly used in various fields, including:\n- Quality control to assess the distribution of product measurements.\n- Clinical research to visualize patient data distributions.\n- Experimental design to summarize and analyze data from experiments, such as tension bond strength in materials testing.\",2\r\n903,RESIDUAL ANALYSIS,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Residual Analysis**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nResidual Analysis is a statistical technique employed to evaluate the adequacy of a statistical model, particularly in the context of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA). Its primary purpose is to assess the goodness of fit of the model by examining the residuals, which are the differences between the observed values and the predicted values generated by the model. This analysis helps identify potential issues with model fit, such as non-linearity, heteroscedasticity, and the presence of outliers.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**  \nIn a typical regression model, the residuals (e_i) are calculated as follows:  \n\\[ e_i = y_i - \\hat{y}_i \\]  \nwhere \\( y_i \\) represents the observed values and \\( \\hat{y}_i \\) denotes the predicted values from the model. The residuals are then analyzed to determine if they exhibit any systematic patterns.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nResidual Analysis does not have a specific design layout as it is a diagnostic tool applied after fitting a statistical model. However, it is commonly used in conjunction with various experimental designs, including completely randomized designs, randomized block designs, and factorial designs.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nFor Residual Analysis to be valid, several assumptions must be met:\n1. Linearity: The relationship between the independent and dependent variables should be linear.\n2. Independence: The residuals should be independent of each other.\n3. Homoscedasticity: The residuals should have constant variance across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. Normality: The residuals should be approximately normally distributed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nResidual Analysis is applicable when assessing the fit of linear models, particularly in the context of ANOVA and regression analysis. It is essential when the researcher needs to validate the assumptions of the model and ensure that the conclusions drawn from the analysis are reliable.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis procedure typically involves:\n1. Calculating the residuals from the fitted model.\n2. Plotting the residuals against the predicted values or independent variables to visually inspect for patterns.\n3. Conducting statistical tests (e.g., Shapiro-Wilk test for normality, Breusch-Pagan test for homoscedasticity) to formally assess the assumptions.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Provides a clear visual representation of model fit issues.\n- Helps identify specific problems such as non-linearity and outliers.\n- Facilitates the refinement of models by highlighting areas for improvement.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- Residual Analysis is primarily focused on linear models and may not be as effective for non-linear models without appropriate transformations.\n- It can be subjective, as visual assessments may vary between analysts.\n- The presence of influential outliers can disproportionately affect the residuals, leading to misleading conclusions.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nResidual Analysis is closely related to other diagnostic methods such as leverage analysis and influence diagnostics (e.g., Cook's distance). Alternative approaches for model assessment include cross-validation and information criteria (e.g., AIC, BIC).\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing Residual Analysis, it is crucial to ensure that the model is appropriately specified before conducting the analysis. Analysts should be cautious of overfitting and should consider using robust statistical techniques if assumptions are violated.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nMany statistical software packages, including R, SAS, SPSS, and Python's statsmodels, provide built-in functions for conducting Residual Analysis, including residual plots and diagnostic tests.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nResidual Analysis is commonly applied in various fields, including agriculture (e.g., analyzing crop yield data), medicine (e.g., assessing treatment effects), and social sciences (e.g., evaluating survey data). It is particularly useful in any scenario where linear regression or ANOVA models are employed to draw inferences from experimental data.\",3\r\n494,RESIDUALS,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Residuals**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nResiduals are defined as the differences between observed values and the values predicted by a statistical model, including regression models and analysis of variance (ANOVA) frameworks. They serve a critical role in assessing the fit and adequacy of the model used in an experiment. By analyzing residuals, researchers can evaluate the variability in the response variable that is not explained by the factors tested, thereby providing insights into the model's performance and the underlying data structure.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nMathematically, residuals (e_i) can be expressed as:\n\\[ e_i = y_i - \\hat{y}_i \\]\nwhere \\( y_i \\) represents the observed value and \\( \\hat{y}_i \\) denotes the predicted value from the model. This formulation is fundamental in regression analysis and other statistical modeling techniques.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nIn the context of experimental designs, residuals are derived from the fitted model based on the design structure, which may include factors, levels, and interactions. The layout of the experiment influences the residuals, as they reflect the unexplained variation after accounting for the effects of the factors included in the model.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe analysis of residuals relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Linearity: The relationship between the independent and dependent variables should be linear.\n2. Independence: Residuals should be independent of each other.\n3. Homoscedasticity: The variance of residuals should be constant across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. Normality: Residuals should be approximately normally distributed, especially for small sample sizes.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nResidual analysis is applicable in various contexts, including regression analysis, ANOVA, and other statistical modeling scenarios. It is particularly useful when assessing model fit, checking assumptions, and diagnosing potential issues with the model. Researchers should consider residual analysis when they suspect that the model may not adequately capture the underlying data patterns.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of residuals typically involves:\n1. Calculating residuals from the fitted model.\n2. Plotting residuals against predicted values or independent variables to visually assess patterns.\n3. Conducting statistical tests (e.g., Shapiro-Wilk test for normality) to evaluate the assumptions of the model.\n4. Identifying outliers or influential points that may affect the model's conclusions.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nThe analysis of residuals provides several advantages:\n- It helps in diagnosing model fit and identifying potential violations of assumptions.\n- Residual plots can reveal patterns that suggest the need for model refinement or transformation of variables.\n- It aids in improving the robustness and reliability of the conclusions drawn from the statistical analysis.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nDespite its strengths, residual analysis has limitations:\n- It may not fully capture complex relationships if the model is overly simplistic.\n- Misinterpretation of residual patterns can lead to incorrect conclusions about model adequacy.\n- The presence of outliers can disproportionately influence residuals, potentially skewing results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nResidual analysis is closely related to various statistical methods, including:\n- Generalized Linear Models (GLMs)\n- Mixed-Effects Models\n- Non-parametric methods for assessing model fit\nAlternatives to residual analysis may include cross-validation techniques and information criteria (e.g., AIC, BIC) for model selection.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing residual analysis, researchers should ensure that the model is appropriately specified and that the assumptions are checked thoroughly. It is also important to consider the context of the data and the potential impact of outliers or influential observations.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nMany statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Python's statsmodels, provide built-in functions for calculating and plotting residuals, facilitating the analysis process.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nResiduals are commonly analyzed in various fields, including:\n- Engineering experiments (e.g., plasma etching, filtration rate studies)\n- Social sciences (e.g., regression analyses in survey data)\n- Biological studies (e.g., assessing treatment effects in clinical trials)\nIn each case, residuals help validate the conclusions drawn from the statistical models employed.\",18\r\n1281,EXTREME RESIDUALS,Extreme residuals are outliers in the residuals of the battery life data that may indicate issues with the model.,1\r\n1816,PLOTTING,\"Plotting refers to the graphical representation of data, such as normal probability plots, which are used to visualize statistical results.\",1\r\n2294,DATA-RECORDING ERROR,,1\r\n914,NORMAL PROBABILITY PLOT,\"### Normal Probability Plot\n\nA Normal Probability Plot (NPP) is a graphical tool utilized to evaluate whether a dataset adheres to a normal distribution. It achieves this by plotting the observed values of the dataset against the expected values derived from a theoretical normal distribution. The primary purpose of the NPP is to visually assess the normality of data, which is a critical assumption in many statistical analyses, including regression and Analysis of Variance (ANOVA).\n\n#### Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure\nThe NPP is constructed by plotting the quantiles of the observed data against the quantiles of a standard normal distribution. If the data follows a normal distribution, the points on the plot will approximately align along a straight diagonal line. Mathematically, for a dataset with n observations, the expected quantiles are calculated using the formula:\n\n\\[ Q_i = \\Phi^{-1}\\left(\\frac{i - 0.5}{n}\\right) \\]\n\nwhere \\( Q_i \\) is the expected quantile, \\( \\Phi^{-1} \\) is the inverse cumulative distribution function of the standard normal distribution, and \\( i \\) ranges from 1 to n.\n\n#### Design Layout or Structure\nThe layout of a Normal Probability Plot consists of two axes: the x-axis represents the theoretical quantiles from the normal distribution, while the y-axis represents the ordered observed values from the dataset. The plot typically includes a reference line (the 45-degree line) against which the plotted points are compared.\n\n#### Assumptions Required for Validity\nThe primary assumption for the validity of a Normal Probability Plot is that the data being analyzed should be independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.). Additionally, the NPP is most effective when the sample size is sufficiently large, as small sample sizes may not provide a reliable assessment of normality.\n\n#### When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria\nThe Normal Probability Plot is applicable in various scenarios, particularly when assessing the normality of residuals in regression models or the distribution of experimental data. It is commonly used in conjunction with other statistical methods, such as Lenth's method, to evaluate the normality of effect estimates. Researchers should consider using an NPP when the normality assumption is critical for the validity of subsequent analyses.\n\n#### Analysis Procedure\nTo create a Normal Probability Plot, the following steps are typically followed:\n1. Order the observed data from smallest to largest.\n2. Calculate the expected quantiles from the standard normal distribution.\n3. Plot the ordered observed values against the expected quantiles.\n4. Assess the alignment of the points with the reference line to determine normality.\n\n#### Advantages and Strengths\nThe Normal Probability Plot offers several advantages:\n- It provides a clear visual representation of data distribution.\n- It is straightforward to construct and interpret.\n- It can reveal deviations from normality that may not be apparent in summary statistics.\n\n#### Limitations and Disadvantages\nDespite its strengths, the NPP has limitations:\n- It may be less effective with small sample sizes, leading to misleading conclusions.\n- The plot can be subjective, as the interpretation of alignment with the reference line may vary among analysts.\n- It does not provide a formal statistical test for normality.\n\n#### Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives\nAlternative methods for assessing normality include the Shapiro-Wilk test, Kolmogorov-Smirnov test, and Anderson-Darling test. Each of these methods provides a formal statistical approach to evaluate normality, which can complement the visual assessment provided by the NPP.\n\n#### Practical Implementation Considerations\nWhen implementing a Normal Probability Plot, it is essential to ensure that the data is appropriately pre-processed, including handling outliers and ensuring independence of observations. The plot should be interpreted in conjunction with other normality tests to provide a comprehensive assessment.\n\n#### Software Implementation\nMany statistical software packages, such as R, Python (with libraries like Matplotlib and SciPy), and SAS, offer built-in functions to generate Normal Probability Plots, making it accessible for practitioners to utilize this tool in their analyses.\n\n#### Common Applications or Examples\nNormal Probability Plots are widely used in various fields, including quality control, clinical trials, and experimental research, to validate the assumptions of normality in residuals and other datasets. For instance, they are frequently employed in the analysis of battery life data and oxide thickness experiments to ensure that the underlying assumptions of statistical models are met.\",9\r\n925,RESIDUAL,,3\r\n1452,BISGAARD,Bisgaard is a researcher who provided a graphical technique called a conditional inference chart to assist in interpreting normal probability plots.,1\r\n1453,CONDITIONAL INFERENCE CHART,The conditional inference chart is a graphical tool designed to help experimenters evaluate effect magnitude for a range of standard deviation values.,1\r\n921,OUTLIER,\"An outlier is a data point that differs significantly from other observations, often requiring careful investigation in statistical analysis.\",4\r\n2716,DIAGNOSTIC CHECKING,\"Diagnostic checking involves assessing the validity of a statistical model, often through residual analysis to ensure the model fits the data appropriately.\",3\r\n922,STANDARDIZED RESIDUALS,\"Standardized residuals are residuals divided by an estimate of their standard deviation, used to identify outliers in regression analysis.\",1\r\n2715,NESTED DESIGNS,\"**NESTED DESIGNS**\n\nNested designs are a specific type of experimental design that involve factors that are nested within other factors. This design structure is particularly useful in situations where the levels of one factor are not fully crossed with the levels of another factor, allowing for a more accurate analysis of variance among groups.\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nThe primary purpose of nested designs is to analyze data where certain factors are inherently hierarchical. For instance, in a nested design, one might have multiple treatments applied within different groups, where each group represents a different level of a higher-order factor. This design helps in accounting for variability among groups and provides a clearer understanding of the effects of the nested factors.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nIn a nested design, the model can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y_{ijk} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\beta_j(i) + \\epsilon_{ijk} \\]\nwhere:\n- \\( Y_{ijk} \\) is the response variable,\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\alpha_i \\) represents the effect of the \\( i \\)-th level of the main factor,\n- \\( \\beta_j(i) \\) is the effect of the \\( j \\)-th level of the nested factor within the \\( i \\)-th level of the main factor,\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ijk} \\) is the random error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nNested designs typically involve a hierarchical structure where one factor is nested within another. For example, if one were to study the effect of different fertilizers (factor A) on various plots of land (factor B), where each plot has its own unique conditions, the plots would be nested within the fertilizer treatments.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of nested designs relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: The data collected from different groups should be independent.\n2. Normality: The residuals should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variances among groups should be approximately equal.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nNested designs are particularly applicable when:\n- The experimental units are organized hierarchically.\n- There is a need to account for variability at different levels of the hierarchy.\n- The researcher is interested in estimating variance components for nested factors.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of nested designs typically involves the use of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) techniques. A nested ANOVA can be performed to partition the total variance into components attributable to the main factor and the nested factor, allowing for hypothesis testing regarding their effects.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Nested designs provide a structured approach to analyzing hierarchical data.\n- They allow for the estimation of variance components, which can improve the efficiency of experiments.\n- They can lead to more accurate conclusions about the effects of factors by accounting for variability at different levels.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- Nested designs can become complex, especially with multiple levels of nesting.\n- They may require larger sample sizes to achieve adequate power for detecting effects.\n- The interpretation of results can be challenging if the nesting structure is not well understood.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nNested designs are related to other hierarchical designs, such as split-plot designs and hierarchical linear models. These alternatives may be considered depending on the specific research question and data structure.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing nested designs, researchers should carefully plan the structure of the experiment to ensure that the nesting is appropriate and that sufficient replication is included at each level. It is also important to consider the randomization of treatments to minimize bias.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nMany statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and SPSS, provide functions for conducting nested ANOVA and estimating variance components. Researchers should familiarize themselves with the specific syntax and options available in their chosen software.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nNested designs are commonly used in agricultural experiments, ecological studies, and clinical trials where treatments are applied within groups that have inherent variability. For example, a study examining the effects of different teaching methods (factor A) on student performance (factor B) within different classrooms (nested factor) would benefit from a nested design approach.\",2\r\n2291,REGRESSOR VARIABLE SPACE,\"The regressor variable space is the multidimensional space defined by the independent variables in a regression model, where the fitted model attempts to approximate the true response surface.\",1\r\n2292,FITTED VALUES,Fitted values are the predicted values generated by a regression model based on the independent variables.,3\r\n2293,PREDICTION ERROR,Prediction error is the difference between the actual observed value and the predicted value for a specific observation in a regression model.,1\r\n923,ANDERSON-DARLING TEST,\"The Anderson-Darling test is a statistical test used to check if a sample of data comes from a specific distribution, particularly the normal distribution.\",2\r\n924,STATISTICAL SOFTWARE PACKAGES,\"Statistical software packages are tools used for statistical analysis, often providing tests for normality and other statistical procedures.\",1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n805,EXPERIMENT,HISTOGRAM,The histogram is used in the experiment to summarize and analyze the bond strength data,143\r\n1014,PLASMA ETCHING EXPERIMENT,RESIDUAL ANALYSIS,Residual Analysis is performed as part of the evaluation of the Plasma Etching Experiment to ensure the model's assumptions are met,44\r\n1039,RESIDUALS,NORMALITY ASSUMPTION,The Normality Assumption is checked using the residuals from the Analysis of Variance.,24\r\n1588,EXTREME RESIDUALS,RESIDUALS,Extreme residuals are identified as part of the residual analysis in the battery life data,19\r\n2389,PLOTTING,RESIDUALS,Plotting residuals is a common practice in statistical analysis to evaluate the fit of a model and check for assumptions,19\r\n3137,RESIDUALS,DATA-RECORDING ERROR,Data-recording errors can lead to unusual residuals that may indicate inaccuracies in the dataset,19\r\n1045,NORMAL PROBABILITY PLOT,RESIDUAL,Residuals are analyzed using normal probability plots to assess the normality of the data distribution,12\r\n1896,BISGAARD,NORMAL PROBABILITY PLOT,\"Bisgaard's work involves interpreting normal probability plots, which are relevant to Lenth's method\",1